WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.070
. OK.

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:05.370
The David Pakman Show subreddit breaking new
records, I think because everybody's home

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.140
and has not that much to do.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:14.599
David Pakman dot com slash read it where nearly
19000 of our viewers and listeners participate

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:20.109
in respectful, moderated political and news
discussions.

00:00:20.109 --> 00:00:21.369
Green blue ninety-eight.

00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:24.920
Posting that the postal service is warning
it may have to.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:30.869
It may have to close as soon as June unless
they get some support.

00:00:30.869 --> 00:00:33.750
You know, what's going on with the postal
service is really horrible.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.530
I had to go into the post office earlier this
week.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.670
Post office here in Boston.

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:41.149
It's very, very ugly.

00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:43.559
It was completely packed.

00:00:43.559 --> 00:00:50.340
People were working and they had some protective
equipment, but they are doing what they can

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.359
to try to mitigate the risk of contagion.

00:00:52.359 --> 00:00:54.649
We, of course, need the postal service.

00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:59.129
But at the same time, the more understaffed
the postal service is, the more people have

00:00:59.129 --> 00:01:00.129
to wait in line.

00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:01.130
I just got the hell out of there.

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:02.250
I mean, I just decided, you know what?

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.409
It's not that important to send this stuff.

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:06.230
And I told people it's not going out right
now.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:09.850
I'm not going to stand in the post office,
you know, for an hour, waiting in line, exposing

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:11.900
myself and and other people.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:16.030
But it's only going to get worse if the postal
service ends up having to shutter completely.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:21.689
We are depending on things being able to be
shipped around related to fighting the virus.

00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:24.200
The postal workers aren't being treated the
right way.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.310
At the same time, this is a service that we
need.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.610
It's a complete and total mess.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:33.730
And of course, the stimulus package includes
no money, no money for the postal service,

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:36.310
a major, major problem.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:41.300
There was a very interesting post by Fidel
Supe on the subreddit asking when the show

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.090
is recorded and when it gets uploaded.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.060
And the answers to those questions are easy.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:50.479
We record in the early afternoon and upload
in the sort of mid to late afternoon.

00:01:50.479 --> 00:01:56.549
But one of our good friends, Brody, who has
called in regularly, put together a sort of

00:01:56.549 --> 00:02:00.969
guide to how to call into the show and be
a good caller.

00:02:00.969 --> 00:02:03.299
And it's an absolutely beautiful guide.

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:06.770
And clearly, Brody also has a lot of time
on his hands.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:11.420
But it's so great because Brody explains,
listen, there is etiquette here that you need

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:13.070
to understand.

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:17.410
If you hear another caller, ask the same thing
you were going to ask.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.270
Think of another question or hang up.

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:23.430
Because two callers in a row with the same
question doesn't make for a good show.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:29.290
Brody points out, Don't be discouraged if
you don't always get your call picked up on.

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:32.850
There are many people on hold and I can only
take so many calls.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:38.010
I love that, Brody says speak clearly and
with a full tone of voice.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:43.410
So David can hear you consider not calling
in when you're going to be moving around in

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.220
a car or whatever.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:46.850
It's not going to be good.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:50.390
And Brody also explains that it's normal to
feel nervous.

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:56.450
But consider writing your question out, which
is an excellent, excellent suggestion.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:02.650
And maybe most importantly, Brody says, get
to your question very quickly.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:08.950
Go right into the question and then listen
to what I say so that we can have like a real

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:09.950
conversation.

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:12.070
Sometimes people have two things written down.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:14.650
No matter what I say to the first one, they
go to the second one.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:17.040
So these are beautiful, beautiful tips from
Brody.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.610
I really appreciate him putting that together.

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:25.440
And I believe that if we all work together,
we will successfully increase the spread of

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.120
good phone calls, good phone calls to the
show.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.170
And I look forward to hearing from more people.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.150
Join the discussion at David Pakman dot com
slash.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:34.599
Read it.

